#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125531} :activity/id #uuid "203caeae-670f-5f01-89d7-10fbe0661dd1" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048635 :topic/id #uuid "b0561e68-388e-5447-b1c3-5f3b61a61231" :topic/math-academy-id 1942} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125531 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125837 :tutorial/content "Given a function $f(x,y),$ how do we calculate the partial derivatives $f_r$ and $f_\\theta,$ where $(r,\\theta)$ are plane polar coordinates?\n\nTo do this, we can use the multivariable chain rule. First, let's write down our tree diagram.\n\n![](images/7f/7f19c4a682b30f1eea1d9c1be7da83bd77ba137173f25eb9c7e5cf253abba015.png)\n\nFrom the tree diagram, we can see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂r} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂r}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂r} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's take the partial derivatives of $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂r} & =\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  &  &  & \\frac{∂y}{∂r} & =\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta } & =-r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  &  &  & \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta } & =r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these results into the multivariable chain rule, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂r} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =-\\frac{∂f}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +\\frac{∂f}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1b053bdf-59d9-5c48-bb1e-43f776bb9b90" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4974 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "683354ae-e60a-5bdb-b374-4caf6c7d6268" :step/math-academy-id 30975 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125532}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125532 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124594 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124405 :question/id #uuid "4940fa22-1584-565f-81fe-dc12e5132f23" :question/math-academy-id "e-8294"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2696e2e-7aa9-5831-9028-9adfe5889bbe" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049940} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050031}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125039} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125040} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360535} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360563} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360564} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360565} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360567}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Cartesian Function With Respect to Plane Polar Coordinates"} :step/id #uuid "216ca278-c544-5255-95a8-27a607e680f3" :step/math-academy-id 30976 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125562}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125533 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124595 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124406 :question/id #uuid "a0246ee6-702f-5d8d-8482-8db1c46b32ed" :question/math-academy-id "e-8295"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "879dfc0f-ea2b-5ccb-bbc6-95f43b9c7073" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125038} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125247} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284539} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360572} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360573} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360574} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360575} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368878} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368885}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Polar Function With Respect to Cartesian Coordinates"} :step/id #uuid "3dba6958-5536-5be1-b625-df1a632c257a" :step/math-academy-id 30977 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125534}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125534 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125838 :tutorial/content "Given a function of three variables $f(x,y,z),$ we can find formulas for $f_r, f_\\theta,$ and $f_z,$ where $(r,\\theta,z)$ are cylindrical polar coordinates. The results that we need are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂r} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =-\\frac{∂f}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +\\frac{∂f}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the first and second equations are identical to the equations derived for plane polar coordinates, as we would expect. \n\nThe difference here is that we now have a third equation that deals with the $z$-dependence. But since the $z$-coordinate is the same in both Cartesian and cylindrical polar coordinates, we can simply differentiate with respect to $z$ as usual.\n\nLet's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "733b56f3-7c9a-59a6-bc6b-ef87bf07d149" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4975 :tutorial/title "The Multivariable Chain Rule With Cylindrical Polar Coordinates"} :step/id #uuid "f4acd445-680f-5872-a4a3-6bda4e7738eb" :step/math-academy-id 30978 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125535}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125535 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124596 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124407 :question/id #uuid "c1e69bbe-b079-56e5-9ce0-296785d73100" :question/math-academy-id "e-8296"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ee45610-e14c-593e-9002-e2b2fcdb4517" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125243} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125248} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360581} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360582} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360583} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367921} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368892}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Cartesian Function With Respect to Cylindrical Polar Coordinates"} :step/id #uuid "19fc48bd-f02a-5fc4-8b9f-23e3d17412b2" :step/math-academy-id 30979 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125563}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125562 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125847 :tutorial/content "Up to now, we've been using the following formulas:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{r} & ={f}_{x}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +{f}_{y}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ {f}_{\\theta } & =-r{f}_{x}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +r{f}_{y}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can treat the above as a system of equations in the \"unknowns\" $f_x$ and $f_y.$ Solving this system gives explicit expressions for $f_x$ and $f_y$ in terms of functions of $r$ and $\\theta.$\n\nFor example, suppose we want an explicit formula for $f_x.$ We can eliminate $f_y$ by multiplying the first equation by $r\\cos\\theta$ and the second equation by $\\sin\\theta$ to give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r{f}_{r}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =r{f}_{x}{cos}^{2}\\theta +r{f}_{y}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta , \\\\ {f}_{\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =-r{f}_{x}{sin}^{2}\\theta +r{f}_{y}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the second equation from the first gives\n\n$$\nr f_r \\cos\\theta - f_\\theta \\sin\\theta = r f_x(\\sin^2\\theta + \\cos^2\\theta),\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\nf_x = f_r \\cos\\theta - \\dfrac1r f_\\theta \\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWe can use a similar procedure to write $f_y$ in terms of functions of $r$ and $\\theta.$ Our results as summarized below:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial x} &= \\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial r} \\cos \\theta\n\t\t\t\t\t\t-\\dfrac{1}{r} \\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial \\theta}\\sin \\theta\\[1ex]\n\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial y} &= \\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial r} \\sin \\theta\n\t\t\t\t\t\t+ \\dfrac{1}{r} \\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial \\theta}\\cos \\theta\n}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e3237863-f115-5396-a03f-fd481d101761" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5358 :tutorial/title "Differentiating of a Function Given in Polar Coordinates With Respect to Cartesian Coordinates"} :step/id #uuid "443067c1-4f4c-574c-818f-cec45ee26f17" :step/math-academy-id 33488 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125533}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125563 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125848 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we have a function $f(x,y,z),$ and we wish to find formulas for $f_\\rho,$ $f_\\theta,$ and $f_\\phi$, where $(\\rho,\\theta,\\phi)$ are spherical polar coordinates.\n\nUsing the multivariable chain rule, it can be shown that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂\\rho } & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}ϕ+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}ϕ+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\operatorname{cos}ϕ, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =-\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\rho \\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}ϕ+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\rho \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}ϕ, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂ϕ} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\rho \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}ϕ+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\rho \\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}ϕ-\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\rho \\operatorname{sin}ϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nYou might want to try deriving these formulas for yourself!" :tutorial/id #uuid "d956ec78-e77b-5d2c-8e7a-97ff2e549005" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5359 :tutorial/title "The Multivariable Chain Rule With Spherical Polar Coordinates"} :step/id #uuid "64b76ae8-f6a1-53fa-b321-5f096bee3ed4" :step/math-academy-id 33489}] :activity/title "The Multivariable Chain Rule With Polar Coordinates" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124734}]}
